C. Application to Search Based Planning
We now consider the applicability of Alg. 3 for heuristic learning in search based planning. Unlike the IPP problem domain, there is no incentive to use a non-stationary policy or imitate Clairvoyant One-step-rewards. Hence we only consider training a stationary policy imitating Clairvoyant Reward-togo. We first need to define a clairvoyant oracle for this problem. Given access to the world map φ, the oracle has to solve for the optimal number of expansions to reach the goal. This allows us to define a clairvoyant oracle planner that employs a backward Dijkstra’s algorithm, which given a world φ and a goal vertex vg plans for the optimal path from every v ∈ V using dynamic programming.
